edb/ref 17592186105840 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105841 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105944 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186105945 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1031 "The AA Similarity Criterion" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1033 1365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050978 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "76bd80f5-fe67-5625-b4da-a9411ae2e552" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520848 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521061 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521100 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521221 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186521323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186521323 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186062419 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d850485a-6c7b-52e0-a49c-5682fa3a56a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064028 #edb/ref 1069 "e-230" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064028 #edb/ref 1071 "An $8\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost is casting a shadow of a $2\\,\\text{m}$ man onto the ground. The man walks away from the lamppost with a speed of $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the man's shadow changing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064028 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "69ba4325-7189-590d-95dd-b11487b632c8" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064028 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/29/29ef832326fff9e5b274c92ae51f2f32a88d463980fd4c109773086c344a76bb.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the man's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = 2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's velocity is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp, and therefore, the distance between him and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8} & =\\frac{y}{2} \\\\ 2x+2y & =8y \\\\ 2x & =6y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} =2.4\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & =\\frac{2.4}{3} \\\\  & =0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the man's shadow increases at a rate of $0.8\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064135 #edb/ref 1069 "e-7829" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064135 #edb/ref 1071 "A $6\\,\\text{m}$ tall street lamp is casting a shadow of a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ woman onto the ground. The woman is walking towards the lamppost with a speed of $1.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ How fast is the length of the woman's shadow decreasing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064135 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "6818c8ab-41e3-546d-a427-304316324bf0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064135 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0b/0bdd4280035b896f113e8d7a27599e488958dae40235faad1658e85710465211.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the woman's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the woman's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the woman's velocity is *negative* because she is walking *toward* the lamp, and therefore the distance between her and the lamp *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =6y \\\\ 1.5x & =4.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1}{3}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{1}{3}x) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{1}{3}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}x}{\\textrm{d}t} = -1.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm{d}y}{\\textrm{d}t} = \\dfrac13 \\cdot (-1.5) = -0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the woman's shadow is decreasing at $0.5\\,\\textrm{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064701 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064703 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521452 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521468 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521526 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521531 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles and Negative Rates" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064292 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "aa831011-1184-5d09-b4e8-b1192443d1b5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186048214 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186049658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050978 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186064727 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521458 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521472 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521477 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186521523 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1039 "Calculating the Rate of Change of a Shadow's Length Using Similar Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186064028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064384 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "c8eb340e-8c49-5478-8cca-a340e9a266e3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186065325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1045 "Calculating Related Rates Using Similar Triangles" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186065324 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048657 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1050 720.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064453 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "80148d75-f74f-5156-96a5-7a61c7ad871b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186062419 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30324" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1071 "A $60\\,\\text{in}$ tall runner is training at night in a park. While running past a lamppost that is $100\\,\\text{in}$ tall, she notices her shadow is growing. Her watch tells her that she is travelling at $50\\,\\text{in/s}.$ How fast is the length of her shadow changing while she runs away from the lamppost?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "47dbce9b-0a44-511c-bf05-d91c2a66fd05" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1073 true #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186064727 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4ef1ba4975846a5375a18dee6cb4a55ba84d22d7c98b19eeaeebbb6b0017b623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the runner's distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of her shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the person's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the runner's velocity is positive because she is running away from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the runner and the lamp increases with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{100} & =\\frac{y}{60} \\\\ 60x+60y & =100y \\\\ 60x & =40y \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{2}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{2}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{2}\\cdot (50) \\\\  & =\\frac{150}{2} \\\\  & =75\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the runner's shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{in/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065181 #edb/ref 1061 29097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065181 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8cd418cf-566e-5476-9114-f2cd4d0ee2f5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065181 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064292 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065324 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065324 #edb/ref 1061 923 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065324 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a20a71f6-b2d9-5750-9e9f-3902758d5232" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065324 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186065386 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065325 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186065181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065325 #edb/ref 1061 924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065325 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "003dba43-750d-5a17-857a-cc7a3e864193" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065325 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186064384 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186065386 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that Bob, who is ![](images/ae/ae1e193fbd577e9009d2601d3f65608af0827a16092c4046543cdb1ccddb9f74.svg) tall, stands in front of a ![](images/a7/a78087a5302fc6677b6ef30a50cfb0b170e3f51b10c25027c0427b938ad21148.svg) street light. How fast does the length of Bob's shadow change if he walks *away* from the street light with a speed of ![](images/0c/0cfc62307d856dbbed71500139ffd3427d49977f140a921ec2f53adb8804b61c.svg)\n\n![](images/0b/0b1f84cc2e31c1a7bdcf2663d8d82f4fecab7dfd12990041b2ba7fd0fe4174bf.png)\n\nWe can solve problems like this using three simple steps.\n\n**Step 1:** Draw a diagram and identify the variables that change with time.\n\n![](images/8d/8d80efb54f0c0f025eaec3cf02f4a1a40cb5a47f1dec4d40e1af63c8e4ac047f.png)\n\nIn the above diagram, we have 