f 17592186045417] [17592186521434 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d49bd1c4-d01f-5541-8a31-35843fff408d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521435 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521435 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521435 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521435 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520839 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521435 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "d6c7671a-533d-5202-b5e2-063b234bf23e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521439 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186520960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521439 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045492 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521439 #edb/ref 1077 "field-1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521439 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186520960 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521439 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "e4acc90d-32b0-5012-bd13-3bc586d538d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521379 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1069 "q-121321" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1071 "A man on a boat is sailing towards a $247\\,\\text{ft}$ high lighthouse. If the height of the man above the sea is $10\\,\\text{ft},$ how fast is the man approaching the lighthouse if his shadow is shrinking at a rate of $0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}?$ Round your answer to one decimal place." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "02ba8f64-b770-56fd-871d-5b790d9828ac" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521452 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/6c/6c85e0c34b34dd9dcb384bb601b9b87a1a6b7174888c9ad49d8c2cd37abc13de.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from the man to the lighthouse, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the man's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the man's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the lighthouse, and therefore the distance between him and the lighthouse *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{247} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =247y \\\\ 10x & =237y \\\\ x & =23.7y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(23.7y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-0.25\\,\\text{mi/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =23.7(-0.25)≈-5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the man's speed is approximately $5.9\\,\\text{mi/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521435 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344484" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1071 "The members of a robotics club are testing a small robot in a courtyard lit by only one large lamp post.  Sam, a $1.4\\,\\text{m}$ student, is walking away from the post, and his shadow is increasing at a rate of $0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.$ If the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than  Sam, how fast is  Sam walking away from the post?\n\nSam's velocity is\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "0f5cbdde-4826-5bb5-88c4-7a602876e76a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521458 #edb/ref 1074 "Since we are given that the lamp post is $7\\,\\text{m}$ taller than Sam, the height of the post is $7+1.4=8.4\\,\\text{m}.$\n\nLet's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/1c/1c17902179842d470f95519599235a9998e4cfbe538a44c201e34c3627c493af.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is Sam's distance from the lamp post, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the length of Sam's shadow changes at the rate\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the rate of change of the length of Sam's shadow is *positive* because he is walking away from the lamp post, and therefore, the length of the shadow *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{8.4} & =\\frac{y}{1.4} \\\\ 1.4x+1.4y & =8.4y \\\\ 1.4x & =7y \\\\ x & =5y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(5y) \\\\  & =5\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=0.8\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =5\\cdot (0.8) \\\\  & =4\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Sam's velocity is $4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521343 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1069 "q-30330" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1071 "Harry shines a flashlight on a $10\\,\\text{in}$ tall cat approaching him. If he is holding the flashlight $30\\,\\text{in}$ above the ground and the animal's shadow is changing at a rate of $11\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is the cat approaching Harry?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "21055998-26d2-5a03-b3c0-e38582a8d839" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521468 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/02/021a84ea0ad36014569028c0ffb39ab9b30f9225cf4bfcccba2d60050754a6f9.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance between Harry and the cat, while $y$ is the length of the cat's shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the velocity of the cat's shadow\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the velocity of the cat's shadow is *negative* because the cat is walking *toward* Harry, and therefore the distance between Harry and the cat *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{30} & =\\frac{y}{10} \\\\ 10x+10y & =30y \\\\ 10x & =20y \\\\ x & =2y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(2y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-11\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =2(-11)=-22\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the speed of the cat is $22\\,\\text{in/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521410 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1069 "q-118674" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1071 "A bus is traveling at night at $50\\,\\text{km/h}.$  After running past a $6.25\\,\\text{m}$ tall lamppost, the bus driver notices that the shadow of the bus is growing. \nIf the height of the bus is $3.75\\,m,$ how fast is the length of the shadow growing while it travels away from the lamp?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "28170d6f-d1bf-54a6-9dab-69ada5f8b07e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521472 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data. \n\n![](images/10/1094154aa39b3d9f1206d23ca920d5717a7f344c4a9486e9c4604752cf0ce623.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the bus' distance from the lamp, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the runner's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the bus's velocity is *positive* because it is moving *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the bus and the lamp *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{6.25} & =\\frac{y}{3.75} \\\\ 3.75x+3.75y & =6.25y \\\\ 3.75x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =1.5x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =1.5\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=50\\,\\text{km/h}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =75\\,\\text{km/h}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the bus' shadow increases at $75\\,\\text{km/h}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521408 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344428" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1071 "A $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall student is jogging away from a $4.0\\,\\text{m}$ parking lot light at $2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the length of the student’s shadow changing?\n\nThe length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2f7c2967-af65-5004-9025-62af739a76dd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521477 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e5/e500e84e039b0f8bef6afc7e636d6142309d65f13017c1f817fdf159b3f4d77c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the student’s distance from the parking lot light, while $y$ is the length of their shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the student’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the student’s velocity is *positive* because they are jogging *away* from the parking lot light, and therefore, the distance between the student and the light *increases* with time. \n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{4.0} & =\\frac{y}{1.5} \\\\ 1.5x+1.5y & =4.0y \\\\ 1.5x & =2.5y \\\\ y & =\\frac{1.5}{2.5}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{15}{25}\\,x \\\\ y & =\\frac{3}{5}\\,x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{3}{5}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=2.4\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{3}{5}\\cdot (2.4) \\\\  & ≈1.4\\,\\text{m/s}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nrounded to one decimal place.\n\nTherefore, the length of the student’s shadow increases at approximately $1.4\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521383 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1069 "q-313985" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1071 "A spotlight $S$ is on the ground $6\\,\\text{m}$ away from a wall and a $1.5\\,\\text{m}$ tall robot is moving towards the wall at a rate of $0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the robot is $2.5\\,\\text{m}$ from the spotlight?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "53b5eaef-0c6b-5313-b037-03a8dbcbc92d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521499 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/e9/e91c4e08aa785de1fe500c2f43e0981501ee597bb7bb042827154842ec06ad0c.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the robot’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the robot’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the robot’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(6-x)+x} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{6} & =\\frac{1.5}{6-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{9}{6-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{9}{6-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{(6-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=6-2.5=3.5\\,\\text{m}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-0.6\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{9}{{2.5}^{2}}\\cdot (-0.6) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $0.9\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521367 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1069 "q-115973" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1071 "A child on a scooter is running away from a $345\\,\\text{cm}$ tall lamppost at $4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.$ The height of the child on the scooter is $96.6\\,cm.$\nHow fast is the length of the child's shadow changing?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "8cb404ed-6af7-5b0d-8507-6193eb5d4f21" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521523 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/fe/fefe072c51da577b504d81ccd1bb3546e77bca9bf9885950ca0c0c2091f9d335.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the child's distance from the lamppost, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the child's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the child's velocity is *positive* because he is running *away* from the lamppost, and therefore, the distance between the child and the lamppost *increases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{345} & =\\frac{y}{96.6} \\\\ 96.6x+96.6y & =345y \\\\ 96.6x & =248.4y \\\\ y & =\\frac{966}{2484}x \\\\ y & =\\frac{7}{18}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{7}{18}x) \\\\  & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=4.5\\,\\text{m/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{7}{18}\\cdot (4.5) \\\\  & =1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the length of the child's shadow increases at approximately $1.75\\,\\text{m/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521439 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344508" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1071 "Ethan is walking toward a $360\\,\\text{in}$ light pole, which has a lamp on top. If Ethan's height is $72\\,\\text{in}$ and his shadow is changing at a rate of $6\\,\\text{in/s},$ how fast is he walking toward the light pole?\n\nEthan is moving at a speed of \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{in/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "91d63402-0442-54f2-9dc3-353df5f34caa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521526 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/4e/4e82a1365099bcf76ae91b75d0d1ad276e4c35f8b172fc60b4d22e9156128d7e.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the distance from Ethan to the light pole, while $y$ is the length of his shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that Ethan's shadow's velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that Ethan's shadow's velocity is *negative* because he is walking *toward* the light pole, and therefore the distance between him and the light pole *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x+y}{360} & =\\frac{y}{72} \\\\ 72x+72y & =360y \\\\ 72x & =288y \\\\ x & =4y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides of the above equation with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(x) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(4y) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t}=-6\\,\\text{in/s}$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t} & =4(-6)=-24\\,\\text{in/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, Ethan's speed is  $24\\,\\text{in/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521434 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1069 "q-344494" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1071 "A bright lantern $L$ is on the ground $18\\,\\text{ft}$ away from a wall and a $6\\,\\text{ft}$ tall actor is moving towards the wall at a rate of $3\\,\\text{ft/s}.$ To **one decimal place**, how fast is the height of the shadow on the wall decreasing when the actor is $8\\,\\text{ft}$ from the lantern?\n\nThe shadow decreases at a speed of approximately \n\n{{field-1}} $\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "98780b5d-6c50-5c2a-867a-a1e2a96de8f6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521531 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's draw a figure according to the given data.\n\n![](images/0a/0a47e801db651c027415a7943e5327bd378c1a08e533dc69673ab4304c8cc7b4.png)\n\nIn the figure, $x$ is the actor’s distance from the wall, while $y$ is the length of the shadow. Both $x$ and $y$ change with time. In particular, we know that the actor’s velocity is\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the actor’s velocity is *negative* because it is moving *toward* the wall, and therefore the distance between it and the wall *decreases* with time.\n\nSince we want to calculate $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t},$ we need to find a relation between $x$ and $y.$ Since the two triangles are similar, the ratios between the corresponding sides are equal, and we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y}{(18-x)+x} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ \\frac{y}{18} & =\\frac{6}{18-x} \\\\ y & =\\frac{108}{18-x}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe differentiate both sides with respect to $t$ using the chain rule, and we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(y) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}t}(\\frac{108}{18-x}) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{(18-x)^{2}}\\cdot \\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, substituting $x=18-8=10\\,\\text{ft}$ and $\\frac{\\text{d}x}{\\text{d}t}=-3\\,\\text{ft/s}$ into the above expression gives, to one decimal place\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & =\\frac{108}{{8}^{2}}\\cdot (-3) \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}t} & ≈-5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the shadow decreases at a speed of approximately  $5.1\\,\\text{ft/s}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
